{
  "id": "Compound::DB00184 - Gene::1143",
  "verbalization": "Nicotine is implicated in the regulation of acetylcholine-activated cation-selective channel activity through its interaction with multiple nicotinic receptor subunits, namely Chrna5, Chrna6, and Chrna3. These subunits participate in the activation of channels that are critical for cellular signaling in response to acetylcholine. The evidence suggests that Nicotine binds to these receptors, triggering a cascade of events that ultimately involves Chrnb4. The shared pathway of acetylcholine-activated channel activity being participated by Chrnb4 across different receptor subunits implies a common functional role in cellular processes, possibly related to ion transport or signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Nicotine influences cellular function through its interaction with nicotinic receptors, with Chrnb4 playing a central role in the downstream effects of this interaction.",
